    "case_summary": "A woman in her 20s with a history of biopsy-proven psoriasis and juvenile dermatomyositis (DM) presented for management of worsening skin condition in 2018, at a time when her juvenile DM had been in remission for several years. Previous treatments included adalimumab, etanercept, methotrexate, guselkumab, ustekinumab, and topical corticosteroids. Her DM had remained stable during treatment with anti-TNF and anti-IL-12/23 medications. After starting new medication, she described fatigue, difficulty picking up her child, and climbing stairs.\n\nPhysical examination revealed:- Diffuse ill-defined erythema and mild edema of the face and eyelids- Violaceous to erythematous patches on the joints, periungual skin, and chest- Increased erythema on chest, dorsal hands, and eyelids- Scaly erythematous plaques on lateral leg- Photodistributed poikiloderma on chest- Slightly erythematous, atrophic papules on the dorsal metacarpophalangeal joints\n\nLaboratory evaluation results were negative or normal, including:- Creatine kinase levels: normal- Aldolase levels: normal",
